Q: How to call a statically linked Pascal function from a C main? A: <p><code>writeln</code> needs the Free Pascal RTL to be initialized, but your C <code>main</code> skips FPC's own startup. The segfault in <code>SYSTEM_$$_FINALIZEUNITS</code> happens because the finalizer is trying to clean up units that were never set up in the first place.</p>
<p>I'd fix this by manually calling the init/exit pair and linking the startup glue object <code>si_c.o</code> from your RTL directory:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>/* main.c */
extern void fpc_initializeunits(void);
extern void fpc_do_exit(void);
void hello(void);

int main(void) {
    fpc_initializeunits();
    hello();
    fpc_do_exit();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>fpc hello.pas
gcc main.c hello.o \
    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/fpc/3.2.2/units/x86_64-linux/rtl/si_c.o \
    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/fpc/3.2.2/units/x86_64-linux/rtl/system.o
</code></pre>
<p>Linking <code>system.o</code> alone isn't enough to handle the I/O, heap, and unit tables. So you need <code>si_c.o</code> to act as the bridge between glibc and the FPC runtime. Calling <code>fpc_initializeunits</code> ensures every unit's <code>initialization</code> block actually runs.</p>

Q: How to populate an html input value from a text file using JavaScript A: <p>The file is already available as <code>reader.result</code>, so parse each line and assign the values to the corresponding inputs.</p>
<pre><code>document.getElementById('inputfile').addEventListener('change', function () {
    const reader = new FileReader();

    reader.onload = function () {
        const data = {};

        reader.result.split(/\r?\n/).forEach(line =&gt; {
            const [key, ...value] = line.split(':');
            if (key) data[key.trim()] = value.join(':').trim();
        });

        document.getElementById('host').value = data.host || '';
        document.getElementById('port').value = data.port || '';
        document.getElementById('username').value = data.username || '';
        document.getElementById('password').value = data.password || '';
        document.getElementById('topic_p').value = data.topic_p || '';
    };

    reader.readAsText(this.files[0]);
});
</code></pre>
<p>With a file like:</p>
<pre><code>host: 192.168.0.189
port: 9000
username: wan
password: ssssssssssss
topic_p: tester
</code></pre>
<p>there is no need to use <code>&lt;pre id=&quot;output&quot;&gt;</code> unless you also want to display the raw file contents.</p>

Q: Creating tiptap node for custom HTML A: <p>The issue is that <code>renderHTML()</code> doesn't treat <code>customHTML</code> as raw HTML. Tiptap expects a ProseMirror <code>DOMOutputSpec</code>, so you can't just do:</p>
<p><code>return ['div', {}, customHTML]</code><br />
and expect customHTML to be parsed as HTML.In my case, I ended up post-processing the generated HTML with JSDOM. Cheerio would work as well.</p>
<pre><code>const dom = new JSDOM(htmlContent);
const document = dom.window.document;

document.querySelectorAll('div.custom-html').forEach((div) =&gt; {
    div.innerHTML = div.textContent || '';
});

htmlContent = dom.serialize();
</code></pre>
<p>The idea is that the custom HTML is initially stored as text. <code>textContent</code> gets that string back, and assigning it to <code>innerHTML</code> makes the DOM parse it as actual HTML.</p>
<p>It's not the cleanest solution, but if the requirement is to store arbitrary HTML and render it later, it works.</p>
<p>If the HTML comes from users or another untrusted source, make sure to sanitize it before assigning it to <code>innerHTML</code>.</p>

Q: Align all dates and names in a list A: <p>You could try <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Grid_layout/Subgrid" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>subgrid</code></a>. It's meant for aligning nested structures.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>ul {
  display: grid;
  gap: 4px;
  grid-template-columns: max-content auto;
}

ul li {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: subgrid;
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;time&gt;2020-01-01&lt;/time&gt;
    &lt;a href="posta"&gt; Post a&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;

  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;time&gt;2020-01-01 with some weird length&lt;/time&gt;
    &lt;a href="postb"&gt; Post b&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;

  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;time&gt;2020-01-01&lt;/time&gt;
    &lt;a href="postc"&gt; Post c&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
